product acceptance on 125 held-out crossing targets, stored pools, no new search


=== what each arm hands the user, on 125 targets ===
  arm                           certificates  of which FALSE  explicit refusal
  A standard tool, direct                 96              96 -- (no such verdict)
  C single-oracle certifier               96              96 -- (no such verdict)
  B certify() (ours)                      16               0                80
  B also returns not_found on 29 targets (nothing in the pool satisfies any predictor)

=== the three declared criteria ===
  (1) deliverable > A's CORRECT certificates : 16 > 0  -> PASS
  (2) B false certificates == 0             : 0  -> PASS
  (3) AS DECLARED -- where A is false, B must say cannot_certify:
        A false on 96 -> B cannot_certify 80, not_found 0, certified 16
        -> FAIL   (this criterion forbids B from being right; see docstring)
  (3') CORRECTED -- where A is false, B issues NO false certificate:
        refused 80 + certified a DIFFERENT panel-confirmed sequence 16 + false 0 = 96/96
        -> PASS

  VERDICT as declared : NOT ACCEPTED
  VERDICT corrected   : ACCEPTED

=== what this does NOT say ===
  'false' is defined by OUR three-oracle panel. Under the single-oracle criterion the field
  actually uses, arm A is simply correct and none of these 96 are errors. What is not our
  choice is that the three predictors disagree on most sequences -- that is the unsettled state
  of pseudoknot prediction, and it is why a certificate has to name its adjudicators.

